  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the first run, using 1 of the 6 runs. It ran all 3 witnesses, and the body reads only `rel.occupies`.\n\n**Body:** `(rises (not occ)) or (rises tt)`, where `occ` is `rel_occupies x r`.\n- `rises (not occ)` is the exit itself: x is off r at this observation and was on r at the one before. Because it fires only on that change, the later observations where x stays off r don't count as exits again. It's unknown when the overlap is unobserved here or at the observation before.\n- `rises tt` covers the first observation of a trace. Without it, the body would say a definite \"false\" there whenever x is on r. The contract says the result is unknown when there is no observation before. This disjunct is false at every other observation, so it changes nothing there. `tt` isn't an atom, so it adds no name the record didn't claim.\n- I chose no readings of my own, so there are no narrowings.\n\n**Witnesses:** r is a lane rectangle from (0,−2) to (20,2), and x is a motor vehicle heading east. Each trace has three scenes 1 s apart, and every scenario is judged at t1 (`window := some (1000, 1000)`).\n- **holds:** x is on r at t0 and off it from t1.\n- **fails:** x stays on r throughout.\n- **blind:** the same layout as holds, with `rel.occupies` masked.\n\nOne shell command was denied: a `sed`/`grep` that combined several operations to look up how `rises` and the three-valued `and` are defined in Core. I read `Foundations/Temporal.lean` with the Read tool instead, so the denial didn't block anything. The only other shell command was the gate.\n\nThe report is in `task_result.md` in the working directory.",
